\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) on mobile and edge devices is reshaping personalized computing, enabling privacy-preserving clinical assistants and real-time on-device coding copilots~\cite{brown2020gpt3,touvron2023llama,devlin2019bert,vaswani2017attention,shi2016edge}. Federated Learning (FL) enables such adaptation without centralizing sensitive user data~\cite{mcm2017fedavg,kairouz2021flsurvey,li2020flspm}. In many deployments, secure aggregation or differential privacy further tightens the effective communication budget~\cite{bonawitz2017secureagg,abadi2016dpsgd}. However, scaling LLM adaptation to cross-device settings collides with a fundamental systems constraint: edge accelerators keep improving local compute, while the uplink remains scarce, time-varying, and highly heterogeneous, effectively dictating end-to-end training latency under synchronous coordination~\cite{lim2020fedcomst,bonawitz2019flsystems}.

\smallskip
\noindent
\emph{The uplink wall and the straggler problem.}
In synchronous federated fine-tuning, uplink heterogeneity turns each round into a straggler-limited process where the slowest participating clients dominate wall-clock time~\cite{bonawitz2019flsystems}. Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) reduces the number of trainable parameters and local memory, but it does not eliminate repeated uplink payloads across rounds~\cite{hu2022lora,dettmers2023qlora}. Under realistic Non-IID client distributions, distinct and non-uniform updates further amplify synchronization delays and client drift, motivating robust federated optimization corrections~\cite{li2020fedprox,wang2020fednova,karimireddy2020scaffold,reddi2021fedopt,acar2021feddyn}. This picture is consistent with cross-device FL benchmarks and stacks (LEAF, FedScale, Flower), where communication frequently dominates system cost in mobile deployments~\cite{caldas2018leaf,hsieh2021fedscale,beutel2020flower}.

\smallskip
\noindent
\emph{Why blind compression is brittle for LLM adaptation.}
A standard remedy is gradient compression via quantization or sparsification~\cite{alistarh2017qsgd,lin2018dgc,reisizadeh2020fedpaq}, often paired with bias correction such as error-feedback~\cite{karimireddy2019errorfeedback}. Yet these parameter-centric schemes are largely uniform or magnitude-driven and effectively perform blind compression that ignores token-level semantic sensitivity. In language, rare tokens such as negations in clinical text or delimiters in code can be decisive for correctness, but uniform policies do not treat them as special. Heuristic token pruning developed for Vision Transformers (e.g., DynamicViT, ToMe)~\cite{rao2021dynamicvit,bolya2022tome} is also risky to transplant to NLP, since low-attention tokens are not necessarily redundant. Inference-time LLM quantization methods reduce storage and execution cost~\cite{dettmers2022llmint8,frantar2022gptq,xiao2023smoothquant,lin2023awq,yao2022zeroquant}, but they do not address the repeated uplink payloads that dominate federated fine-tuning.

\smallskip
\noindent
\emph{Our approach: Fisher-guided communication control.}
To dismantle the uplink wall, we propose \textsc{Fed-FSTQ} (\underline{F}isher-\underline{S}pectrum-aware \underline{T}oken \underline{Q}uantization). To the best of our knowledge, \textsc{Fed-FSTQ} is the first framework to leverage Fisher information as a dynamic communication control primitive for federated LLM fine-tuning, rather than treating curvature only as an optimization aid. Curvature-aware signals have long motivated stable optimization (e.g., natural gradient and K-FAC)~\cite{amari1998natural,martens2015kfac} and sensitivity-based pruning under loss-preserving approximations~\cite{lecun1989obd,hassibi1993obs,kirkpatrick2017ewc,lee2019snip,wang2020grasp}. Recent systems increasingly pursue lightweight curvature proxies that are compatible with modern workloads and constrained devices. Following this direction, we use a token-level Fisher proxy to decide where limited uplink resources should be spent during local fine-tuning. \textsc{Fed-FSTQ} estimates semantic sensitivity online and couples (i) importance-aware token selection with (ii) mixed-precision quantization, allocating high-fidelity bandwidth to load-bearing evidence while suppressing redundant updates. The resulting update improves the signal-to-noise ratio of aggregated adapter updates under Non-IID heterogeneity.

\smallskip
\noindent
\emph{Drop-in deployability.}
\textsc{Fed-FSTQ} is a drop-in module for standard federated PEFT pipelines (e.g., FedAvg+LoRA)~\cite{mcm2017fedavg,hu2022lora}. It requires no change to server-side aggregation and remains compatible with practical deployment constraints, including secure aggregation and differential privacy~\cite{bonawitz2017secureagg,abadi2016dpsgd}.

\smallskip
\noindent
\emph{Results and contributions.}
We evaluate \textsc{Fed-FSTQ} on multilingual QA (Fed-Aya) and medical QA (Fed-Med)~\cite{jin2019pubmedqa,jin2020medqa} using a virtual edge testbed that emulates heterogeneous 4G/LTE uplinks. \textsc{Fed-FSTQ} reduces cumulative uplink traffic to reach a fixed quality threshold by 46$\times$ relative to Fed-LoRA and improves straggler-limited time-to-accuracy by 52\%. On NVIDIA Jetson edge devices, Fisher-derived masks can also be reused to accelerate inference, yielding a 1.55$\times$ speedup.

The main contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \emph{Fisher-guided federated communication control.} We formulate token-level Fisher sensitivity as a communication control primitive for federated LLM fine-tuning, enabling mixed-precision transmission that preserves semantically decisive evidence under severe uplink constraints.
    \item \emph{System-level gains under heterogeneity.} We demonstrate substantial reductions in cumulative uplink traffic (46$\times$) and straggler-limited time-to-accuracy (52\%) under realistic Non-IID client distributions and heterogeneous mobile uplinks.
    \item \emph{Edge deployability and dual-use benefits.} We validate feasibility on real edge hardware (NVIDIA Jetson), showing that Fisher estimation overhead is amortized by communication savings and that learned masks benefit both training communication and inference efficiency.
\end{itemize}


